# Metal-Organic Framework Templated Inorganic Sorbents for Rapid and Efficient Extraction of Heavy Metals

## abstract
An innovative wet-treatment with Na2 S transforms two indium metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) into a series of porous inorganic sorbents. These MOF-templated materials display remarkable affinity for heavy metals with saturation occurring in less than 1 h. The saturation capacity for Hg(II) exceeds 2 g g(-1) , more than doubling the best thiol-functionalized sorbents in the literature.

Environmental contamination of water with mercury and other heavy metals is a source of mounting concern for global public health, 1  as primary anthropogenic emissions far surpass geogenic sources. 2  To minimize the amount of heavy metals released into the environment, the development of selective solid-phase sorbents is an essential pursuit for industrial applications and environmental remediation. Thiolgrafted mesoporous silica, 3  thiol-functionalized self-assembled monolayers on mesoporous silica (SAMMS), 4  sulfur-functionalized mesoporous carbon (S-FMC), 5  humic-acid functionalized $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles, 6  and porous chalcogels have all been investigated for extraction of $\mathrm{Hg(II)}$ from water. 7  More recently, exciting results have been obtained using several variations of layered metal sulfides (KMS-1, LHMS-1) 8  or layered double hydroxides with intercalated polysulfides $(\mathrm{S}_{x}\mathrm{-LDH})$ . 9 Finally, metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) decorated with thiols 10  or thiol ethers 11  have also been investigated for these applications. Unfortunately, slow sorption kinetics or low sorption capacity significantly diminish the practical utility for all the aforementioned materials. The ideal sorbent must combine porosity, binding-site accessibility, and homogeneous functionalization to achieve both rapid sorption and high saturation capacity.
Herein we report the preparation of four porous indium sulfide materials by treating two indium MOF precursors with aqueous and methanolic solutions of $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ (Scheme 1). This simple wet-treatment process allows rapid and scalable sorbent preparation under mild conditions, without costly carbonization, complicated ligand syntheses, or challenging activation steps. Utilization of a MOF template allows both porosity and desirable sorbent functionalities to be imparted, while simultaneously affording a means of influencing the morphology of the final material. These novel materials were investigated for sorption of the heavy metals $\mathrm{Hg(II)}$ , Pb(II), and Cd(II) from aqueous solutions. While performing well for $\operatorname{Cd}(\operatorname{II})$ and ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{II}})$ extraction, the MOF-templated materials more than doubled the best saturation capacity of any mercury sorbent reported in the literature, rapidly extracting greater than twice their mass in $\mathrm{Hg(II)}$ . With $_{0.1\mathrm{~g~}}$ sorbent, $1\mathrm{~L~}$ of water containing $150~\mathrm{ppm}$ $\mathrm{Hg(II)}$ could be $99.7\%$ decontaminated after just 1 h of contact.
A new microwave synthesis was developed to rapidly prepare large quantities of the nanoscale MOF MIL-68, while $\mathrm{MIL}{-}53{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ was prepared as described in the literature. 12
Framework formulas for Mil-68 and Mil- $53\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ are $\mathrm{In}(\mathrm{BDC})$ (OH) and $\mathrm{In}(\mathrm{BDC}{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2})(\mathrm{OH}$ ) ( $\mathrm{\DeltaBDC}~=~$ benzene dicarboxylate; BDC- $\mathrm{\DeltaNH}_{2}~=$ amino-benzene dicarboxylate), respectively. Characterization by transmission electron microscopy (TEM) revealed nanorod morphology for both MOFs (Figure 1a, Figure S1, Supporting Information), while powder X-ray diffractometry (PXRD) confirmed the topology and crystallinity of the MOF structures (Figure S2 and S3, Supporting Information). Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) revealed a precipitous, characteristic weight loss at $500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (MIL-68) and $400~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (MIL$53\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2})$ ), indicative of thermal decomposition of the organic bridging ligand and formation of $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ (Figure S4, Supporting Information).
The porous inorganic sorbents were obtained by immersing the MOF templates in aqueous or methanolic 13  solutions containing 5 equivalents of $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ . Henceforth, samples are differentiated by the number of their MOF template followed by the solvent in which they were treated (e.g., the material prepared by treating MIL-68 in water is 68(W), while treating $\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}53{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ in methanol is 53(M)). TEM images revealed the structure of the MOF template was largely retained, regardless of treatment solvent, while PXRD studies confirmed the materials were amorphous after treatment. Images of 68(W) and 68(M) revealed discrete particles possessing irregular surface features, which appear to be multiple nanorods sharing a long edge via a self-assembly process (Figure 1b,c). In contrast, MIL- $53\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ materials formed aggregates of trigonal-shaped nanoparticles, presumably due to the aggregation of the MOF precursor (Figure S5 and S6, Supporting Information). Analysis by dynamic light scattering (DLS) confirmed the reduction in particle size for all materials (Figure S7 and S8, Supporting Information). Importantly, $\zeta.$ -potential measurements revealed a dramatic difference in surface potential following $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ treatment: MOF precursors possessed positive surface potential of $16.23~\mathrm{mV}$ and $0.36~\mathrm{mV}$ for MIL-68 and $\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}53{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ respectively, while the treated materials were highly negative, ranging from $-34.9\ensuremath{\mathrm{\mV}}$ to $-38.2\mathrm{\mV}$ (Table 1). Control experiments involving simple precipitation of $\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{Na}_{\mathrm{y}}\mathrm{In}{\mathrm{S}}_{2}$ from $\mathrm{In}({\mathrm{NO}}_{3})_{3},$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ , and $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ resulted in materials with no discernible morphology (Figure S9, Supporting Information) and large polydispersity (Figure S10, Supporting Information). These differences demonstrate the importance of using a MOF template and also establish MOF dissolution and subsequent precipitation is not the mechanism for material formation.
TGA curves of the materials post-treatment were devoid of the precipitous weight loss at the MOF decomposition temperature, indicative of complete ligand exchange and formation of a purely inorganic material. After loss of approximately $5-6~\mathrm{wt\%}$ coordinated solvent $(<200^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ , the sulfide-treated materials displayed a weight gain of $6{-}10~\mathrm{wt}\%$ at approximately $350~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{i}}$ followed by a weight loss around $500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Figure S11, Supporting
Information). The weight gain is due to initial sulfur oxidation, and the subsequent weight loss corresponds to further sulfur oxidation to release $\mathrm{SO}_{2}$ and form $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ Differential scanning calorimetry showed endothermic events at 307, 361, and $510~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , correlating with significant mass changes observed by TGA (Figure S12, Supporting Information). Variable-temperature
PXRD measurements (Figure S13, Supporting Information) revealed transition of the materials through three crystallographic phases, initially forming phase segregated $\mathrm{NaInS}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ at low temperatures, existing exclusively as $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ at intermediate temperatures, and finally oxidizing to $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ above $450^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
Composition analysis of Na and In was performed by ICP-OES, resulting in the formulas displayed in Table 1 for sulfide-treated MOFs. Furthermore, the presence of surface thiol functionalities was confirmed by FTIR where weak S–H stretching vibrations were observed in all materials around $2400~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ (Figure S15, Supporting Information). TGA curves of the materials prepared analogously by treatment of MOF templates with NaOH in water and methanol did not show any gain in mass at elevated temperatures, supporting the formation of $\mathrm{H_{x}N a_{y}I n S_{z}}$ materials instead of indium oxides for the sulfide-treated MOFs (Figure S14, Supporting Information). Surface-area measurements were obtained by $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ isotherms and analyzed with the Brunauer–Emmett– Teller (BET) equation (Table 1 and Figure S16, Supporting Information). It is evident that different crystal structures of the templates significantly influence the resulting materials. MIL-68 possesses a Kagomé-like lattice 12a  and produced materials with higher surface areas than MIL- $53\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ composed of 1D tunnels with rhombus-shape apertures. 12c 14 Treatment with water or methanol had minimal influence on the final surface areas.
Given the porosity and high sulfur content of these materials, we investigated them for use in extracting heavy metals from aqueous solutions. Sulfide-treated MOFs, crystalline $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ crystalline $\mathrm{NaInS}_{2}$ and MOF controls were added at a phase ratio of $0.1\mathrm{mg/mL}$ with a contact time of $^{1\mathrm{h}}$ . Isotherms were obtained by varying the concentration of $\mathrm{Hg(II)}$ in solution and measuring the subsequent sorption. The MOF-templated materials exhibited a dramatic color change upon prolonged exposure to $\mathrm{Hg(II)}$ solutions, turning black as they approached saturation (Figure 1d, inset). While modest sorption was observed for the crystalline $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ and $\mathrm{NaInS}_{2}$ materials, neither MOF precursor had any detectable sorption capacities. The sorption data were fitted using the Langmuir model, 15  expressed linearly as:
$$
\frac{C_{\mathrm{e}}}{q_{\mathrm{e}}}=C_{e}\frac{1}{q_{\mathrm{max}}}+\frac{1}{K_{\mathrm{L}}q_{\mathrm{max}}}
$$
where $q_{\mathrm{e}}$ is the sorption capacity at equilibrium, $q_{\mathrm{max}}$ is the saturation capacity of the sorbent, $C_{\mathrm{e}}$ is the $\mathrm{Hg(II)}$ concentration in solution at equilibrium, and $K_{\mathrm{L}}$ is the Langmuir constant. As displayed in Figure 1d and Figure S17 and Table S1 in the Supporting Information, the Langmuir model fit the experimental data particularly well, with all but one $R^{2}$ value greater than 0.990. Fits performed with Freundlich 16  and Temkin 17 isotherms (Table S2 and S3 and Figure S18–S19, Supporting Information) had lower $R^{2}$ values for all sulfide-treated MOFs, indicating they are less representative of the observed sorption process. The material 68(W) was determined to have a saturation capacity of almost $2.2\ \mathrm{g}$ of $\mathrm{Hg(II)}$ per gram of sorbent, more than 10 times higher than the best MOF, 10  3 times better than S-FMC, 5  and more than doubling the best sorption obtained with mesoporous silica. 18  Distribution coefficient $(K_{\mathrm{d}})$ values, a mass-weighted partition coefficient representing a sorbent material's affinity for a sorbate, were calculated based on the obtained sorption data. The $K_{\mathrm{d}}$ values are defined by the equation:
$$
K_{\mathrm{d}}=\left({\frac{C_{\mathrm{i}}-C_{\mathrm{e}}}{C_{\mathrm{e}}}}\right)\times{\frac{V}{m}}
$$
where $C_{\mathrm{i}}$ is the initial concentration of metal, $C_{\mathrm{e}}$ is the concentration at equilibrium, $V$ is the volume of solution in mL, and $m$ is the mass in grams of sorbent used. $K_{\mathrm{d}}$ values above $10^{4}$ are generally regarded as very good, while values above $10^{5}$ are exceptional. 5,19  All the MOF-templated materials have $K_{\mathrm{d}}$ values above $2\times10^{5}\mathrm{~mL~g^{-1}}$ with 68(W) having a $K_{\mathrm{d}}$ greater than $6.8\times10^{6}\mathrm{mLg^{-1}}$ (Table 1).
It is worth noting in addition to high saturation capacities and $K_{\mathrm{d}}$ values, the MOF-templated materials also possessed extremely rapid saturation, as the contact time for each experiment was $^{1\mathrm{~h~}}$ . This is in stark contrast to the lengthy contact times required for other porous materials, which routinely range from $12\mathrm{~h~}$ to as much as one week. It is well established that the performance of a porous sorbent material is dictated by bonding strength and accessibility of binding sites. 20  We attribute the high saturation capacities and rapid sorption to the synergistic effects arising from homogeneous distribution of soft sulfur atoms, coupled with nanopores allowing rapid diffusion of $\mathrm{Hg(II)}$ throughout the material. The crystalline $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ and $\mathrm{NaInS}_{2}$ had only modest sorption properties despite homogeneous sulfur distribution, while the MOF precursors had no sorption despite large porosities. While it is expected the negative surface charge would attract cationic $\mathrm{Hg(II)}$ to accelerate sorption, $\mathrm{Hg(II)}$ speciates to neutral ${\mathrm{Hg}}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ at the $\mathrm{\pH}$ investigated in this study, and would thus not contribute directly to the rapid sorption. 18,21
ICP-MS analysis of solutions after sorption yielded concentrations of In statistically equivalent to that of the control blank, indicating $\mathrm{Hg(II)}$ was not exchanging for In and suggesting covalent bond formation with S. The physical binding of $\mathrm{Hg(II)}$ throughout the 68(W) was confirmed by Raman spectroscopy and extended X-ray absorption fine structure (EXAFS) studies. In the Raman spectra, the characteristic $_\mathrm{S-H}$ stretching frequency just below $2500~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ can be clearly observed in the as-prepared 68(W) material. Upon saturating with $\mathrm{Hg(II)}$ , this band is completely removed. While a strong band at $355~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ consistent with $\mathrm{Hg-S}$ stretching may be present, the broad In-S stretching frequency in the same region makes definitive assignment impossible (Figure S20 and S21, Supporting Information).
Extended X-ray abosrption fine structure (EXAFS) analysis was performed at the $\mathrm{{Hg}\ L_{\mathrm{III}}}$ absorption edge $(12284~\mathrm{eV})$ to investigate the coordination environment of $\mathrm{Hg(II)}$ on 68(W). The initial study of $\mathrm{Hg(II)}$ sorption on mesoporous silica by EXAFS suggested $1{:}1\ \mathrm{Hg}$ bonding to sulfur with terminating hydroxide functions, 3b  while a subsequent study suggested multiple layers of $\mathrm{Hg}$ bonding with bridging oxygen. 22  Most recently a pair distribution function (PDF) study revealed no detectable Hg-O bond formation, even at high $\mathrm{\sf{Hg}}$ concentrations. The detection of $\mathrm{Hg-}\mathsf{S}$ and $\mathrm{Hg\mathrm{-}H g}$ atomic pairs revealed clustering of $\mathrm{\sfHg}$ centers upon sorption, and it was proposed S binds two $\mathrm{Hg}$ neighbors and forms extended cationic $-\mathrm{Hg-RS-Hg-}$ chains through bridging thiolate ligands. 23
EXAFS analysis provides some insight to the binding mode of $\mathrm{Hg(II)}$ in these materials. In contrast to previous publications, incorporation of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Hg-O}}$ scattering paths added unnecessary amplitude at small $\chi(k)$ and diminished the quality of the fit, 22  however, a good fit (R-factor $=0.01$ , indicating ca. $1\%$ misfit) was obtained through inclusion of multiple S singlescattering paths (Figure 1e and Table S6, Supporting Information). Allowing the number of coordinating S atoms to vary as a free parameter resulted in a fit with average $\mathrm{Hg(II)}$ coordination environment including $3.0\pm0.3\mathrm{~S~}$ atoms, suggesting formation of a specific binding site composed of three equidistant S atoms. We also attempted to include second sphere $\mathrm{Hg(II)}$ single-scattering paths, consistent with $\mathrm{Hg(II)}$ cluster model proposed by PDF studies. 23  Fixing the number of first shell S at 3 and allowing the number of second shell $\mathrm{Hg(II)}$ to vary yielded a result consistent with 0. This is not unexpected, as EXAFS structural information diminishes rapidly as a function of distance from the scattering atom, and is known to be especially true for analysis of the $\mathrm{{Hg}\ L_{\mathrm{III}}}$ edge. The first coordination sphere of $\mathrm{Hg(II)}$ is often the only one that can be fit with statistical reliability. 22–24
Upon saturation, the ratio of $\mathrm{Hg{:}}\mathrm{S}$ in $53(\mathbb{W})$ and 53(M) was approximately 2:3, while for 68(W) $\mathrm{Hg(II)}$ was present at an approximately 1:1 ratio. A similar phenomenon has been observed in different sorbent materials, proposed to be the result of S donating each lone pair of electrons to a different $\mathrm{Hg(II)}$ atom 5  or from formation of polymeric $\mathrm{Hg(SR)^{+}}$ species where each S interacts with $2{-}3\ \mathrm{Hg(II)}$ atoms. 23  In the latter case, the negative surface charge may contribute significantly to the rapid $\mathrm{Hg(II)}$ sorption by eliminating the necessity for charge balancing counterions to diffuse to the surface of the material.
Sorption isotherms for ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{II}})$ and $\operatorname{Cd}(\operatorname{II})$ were also investigated with all MOF-templated materials, achieving saturation capacities as high as $139\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathsf{C d}\upmathrm{g}^{-1}$ and $254\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for 68(W) as determined by fitting with the Langmuir model (Figure S26 and S27 and Table S8–S13, Supporting Information). Cd(II) and Pb(II) sorption revealed materials prepared in water had approximately twice the saturation capacity of those prepared in methanol, which was not observed for $\mathrm{Hg(II)}$ sorption.
Interestingly, treatment solution is significantly more important than surface area, suggesting water facilitates formation of appropriate binding sites.
While the reduction in a mass-based saturation capacity is intuitive, conversion to $\upmu\mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ reveal approximately equivalent moles of ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{II}})$ and $\operatorname{Cd}(\operatorname{II})$ are sorbed, with a 4–9-times improvement for $\mathrm{Hg(II)}$ sorption across all materials. This significant enhancement in $\mathrm{Hg(II)}$ sorption can be attributed to the extremely low $K_{\mathrm{sp}}$ value for $\mathrm{Hg}\mathrm{S}$ $(2\times10^{-52})^{[25]}$ compared with those for $\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{S}$ and CdS $(8\times10^{-28}$ and $8\times10^{-27}$ respectively), 26 in conjunction with the high density of specific binding sites on the surface of the inorganic materials, resulting in the formation of bridging polymeric $\mathrm{Hg(SR)^{+}}$ species. While similar EXAFS data is not available for ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{II}})$ and $\operatorname{Cd}(\operatorname{II})$ , they are expected to bind to individual S atoms rather than as polymers, with dense hydration spheres inhibiting sorption on adjacent binding sites. 27
In conclusion, we have developed a novel wet-treatment process to transform sacrificial MOF templates into porous, robust inorganic materials containing surface thiol functions which are inaccessible by previously published pyrolytic techniques. These indium-sulfide-based materials are highly efficient sorbents for heavy metals, possessing rapid sorption kinetics and surpassing all previously reported thiol-functionalized mesoporous materials in $\mathrm{Hg(II)}$ extraction. Fitting EXAFS spectra suggests each $\mathrm{Hg(II)}$ is bound cooperatively by three S atoms, forming a cationic polymer on the surface. This innovative MOF templating method shows great promise for the preparation of highly robust and selective sorbents for a wide range of challenging separations.
## Experimental Section
Microwave Synthesis of MIL-68: MIL-68 was prepared by combining $408.2~\mathsf{m g}$ ( $\left\langle1.05\:\:\:\mathsf{m m o l}_{,}^{\prime}\right.$ ) $\mathsf{I n}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}x\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ with $175m g$ $(7.05~\mathsf{m m o l})$ terephthalic acid in $5\ m L$ of $N,N^{\prime}$ -dimethylformamide. This solution was heated by microwave in sealed teflon vessles at $400~\mathbb{W}$ for $15\mathrm{\min}$ at $\mathsf{l o o\mathsf{\Lambda}^{\circ}C}$ with stirring. After cooling, the white solid was isolated by centrifugation, washed with water several times, then dispersed in water for storage until use. Yield: $272.5~\mathrm{mg}$ ( $(0.92\ m m\circ l)_{,}$ ; $86.7\%$ .
Synthesis of Sorbents: $50~\mathsf{m g}$ of the MOF template $(0.169~\mathsf{m m o l}$ , 0.161 mmol for MIL-68, MIL- $53\ –\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ respectively) were isolated by centrifugation, then suspended in $70~\mathsf{m L}$ of deionized water or distilled methanol containing 5 equivalents of ${\mathsf{N a}}_{2}{\mathsf{S}}$ . The solution was agitated overnight, precipitate collected by centrifugation, washed with water, and stored in water until use. 68(W) Yield: $31.0\mathrm{mg}$ (0.151 mmol) $89.3\%$ 68(M) Yield: $32.5~\mathsf{m g}$ (0.137 mmol) $81.7\%$ . 53(W) Yield: $35.8~\mathsf{m g}$ (0.158 mmol) $98.1\%$ . 53(M) Yield: $_{13.8\mathrm{~mg}}$ $(0.067~\mathrm{mmol}$ ) $39.8\%$ .
X-ray Absorption Spectroscopy (XAFS): XAFS data were collected at the $\mathsf{H g\ L_{\mathsf{I I I}}}$ -edge (12284 eV) at Beamline 13-BM-D at the Advanced Photon Source (APS) at Argonne National Laboratory. Data were processed and analyzed using the Athena and Artemis programs of the IFEFFIT packge based on FEFF 6. 28  The $\mathsf{H g O}$ standard was fitted using the single-crystal model structure, yielding the $S_{0}{}^{2}$ parameter which was fixed for fits of $\mathsf{H g S}$ and $\mathsf{H g}(\mathsf{I I})$ adsorbed on 68(W) (i.e., $\mathsf{H g}@68(\mathsf{W}))$ . $\mathsf{H g S}$ was fitted with a combination of the scattering paths from cinnabar and metacinnabar by scaling the $S_{0}{}^{2}$ parameter by a variable, as it is directly correlated to path degeneracy in the EXAFS equation. The first coordination sphere of $\mathsf{H g}(\mathsf{I I})$ as $\mathsf{H g@68(w)}$ was fitted by allowing the number of coordinating sulfur to vary as a free parameter. Additional details are available in the Supporting Information.
